WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.212
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.950
KYLE STORY: Well, thank
you everyone for coming.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:12.990
We appreciate you coming out
and joining this session.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:13.930
I'm Kyle Story.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.170
I'm a computer vision
engineer at Descartes Labs.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.910
And today, I want to tell you
about how computers see Earth,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.620
a machine learning approach
to understanding satellite

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.280
imagery at scale.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.660
And so there's a few
things that I'm hoping,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.410
depending where you're
coming to this talk

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.780
from, that you might take away.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.600
First, I'm hoping that
everybody will understand

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.200
how we've been able to build
a platform within the Google

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.380
Cloud, how that's
allowed us to provide

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.960
an efficient development
environment for building

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.970
machine learning
models and then scale

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.130
those models across
large data sets

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:51.010
to be able to understand
the entire globe.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:55.000
Second, I'm hoping that you will
take away the types of problems

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.130
that we've found
compelling and that we've

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:00.130
been able to address using
satellite imagery and machine

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:01.450
learning techniques.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.040
And then third,
hopefully get you

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.599
thinking about the
types of problems

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:07.660
that you might be able to
address with these data--

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.700
with this type of
infrastructure and how

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.180
you might be able to address
some of your business problems,

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.270
whether that's monitoring
infrastructure,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.330
supply chains, agriculture,
even understanding

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.700
the natural ecosystem.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.940
So the world is
teeming with activity,

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.270
whether that's manmade
infrastructure, like here

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.680
in the zoom in sports
stadium inside of a city;

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.390
or that's agriculture,
shown here,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.660
with crop circles
in South America;

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.634
or the natural ecosystem.

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:41.800
There are things happening
all over the entire globe

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.020
all the time, and
satellite imagery

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.910
is a particularly
effective way of being

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:48.550
able to zoom in and
understand what is

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.110
going on all across the globe.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.011
So there's a big
dynamic range in what we

00:01:53.011 --> 00:01:54.260
want to be able to understand.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:57.910
We want to zoom all the
way in to the human scale

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.520
of a few meters,
or a meter or less.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.260
And then we
simultaneously want to be

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.026
able to extend these
analyses across countries

00:02:05.026 --> 00:02:05.650
and continents.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.880
So it presents a big challenge.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.030
In satellite imagery,
we are at the beginning

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.090
of what we think of
as a regime change

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.920
in simply just the data set.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.150
So previously, satellites
were big and expensive,

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.780
costing over $100
million, weighing a ton.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.730
Companies and governments
could only send up

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.540
a few of these at a time.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.600
And so while these
satellites provide

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.910
excellent and
incredible information,

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.950
you can't monitor
the entire globe

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.500
with any sort of
regular cadence.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.890
With the advent of CubeSats
and smaller satellites,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:42.940
that's really changing.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.640
So now, companies are
able to build satellites

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:50.050
for $60,000, $70,000 a
piece, these small things.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.060
And then, they can
launch entire fleets up

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.390
into the sky to be able
to effectively monitor

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.680
the entire globe
almost in real time.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.520
And so what we're looking
at for the near future is

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.880
whereas the data that
underlies Google Earth

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.370
takes approximately
two years to complete

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.160
a new picture of
the entire world,

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.200
we expect that within
around five years,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.440
private satellites will be
able to cover the entire globe

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.860
every 20 minutes.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.250
So this is a completely
different class

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.070
of the types of problems
that you can address and then

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.610
also a completely different
regime for the big data

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.080
that you need to
deal with in order

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.570
to make sense of all
of these information.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.330
So Descartes, in order to
make sense of these data,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.916
we're building a living
digital atlas of the globe.

00:03:35.916 --> 00:03:38.040
And so during this talk,
I wanted to tell you a bit

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.290
about that and then how
we're using machine learning

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.850
to understand the value in the
information in these pictures.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:47.850
So we can think about
this as a classic big data

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.710
challenge with the three Vs--

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.210
Volume, Variety, and Velocity.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:55.170
And so in this context, volume
is what it usually means,

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.820
the massive amount
of data that we

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:00.690
need to be able to
pull in and work with.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.300
Variety, in this case, means
the different types of data

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.850
that we want to
synthesize into analyses,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.220
and I'll talk about all
these in more detail.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.140
And velocity here, I
really mean two things.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.370
I mean first, being able
to access all of that data

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.300
efficiently but then also
being able to analyze and scale

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.149
your analysis across the globe
rather than having someone

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:23.950
look at individual pictures.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:26.410
So to kind of work through
those with some numbers

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.740
and a bit of detail,
global satellite data sets

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.360
are very big.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.000
This composite that
we made is comprised

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.870
of 3.1 trillion pixels per band,
and the Landsat satellite that

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:45.220
took this has collected over
70 trillion pixels between 2013

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.330
and 2017.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:50.200
So that comprises 320
terabytes on disk,

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.390
and that's just one
satellite among many.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:57.580
So we've processed around
10 to 12 terabytes of data--

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.172
sorry, petabytes of data onto
our platform at this point.

00:05:01.172 --> 00:05:03.130
And this is just going
to expand exponentially.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.300
But just to give you a bit of an
image in your head of what that

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.820
looks like, let's take one
satellite, the Sentinel

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:12.660
Satellite, and take all of
the picture that it's taken

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.090
and print each individual band
on a one-millimeter thick sheet

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:16.600
of paper.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.580
Stack that up, and
that would form

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.610
a column that's 410
kilometers tall, so

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.850
from the surface of the Earth
up into the thermosphere.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.430
So it's a lot of data.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.560
The data variety that
we're working with

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.750
comes from different
types of sensors

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.790
that we want to be able to work
with and synthesize together.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.150
So here on the left,
there are passive sensors,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.420
like normal pictures
that you would take.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.190
In the middle, there
are active sensors,

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:46.490
which actually send
down radiation or radar,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.335
and then receive the
signal and interfere

00:05:48.335 --> 00:05:51.860
it to get 3D information about
the surface of the Earth.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.260
That's called synthetic
aperture radar.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.300
And then, there's
near-infrared data

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.940
that actually measures
the thermal emission

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.350
from sources on the ground.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.930
And then, there's all
kinds of other georelevant

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:06.320
data like weather, cell
phones, derived products,

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.400
derived mass, customer data.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.440
So we want to be able to put all
of this into the Cloud in a way

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.650
that customers can access
in an efficient way

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:20.700
to be able to build these
types of analysis algorithms.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.970
And then velocity-- so
thinking about the--

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:28.140
how long it takes to work
with these types of data,

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:30.150
let's consider again
the Sentinel Satellite.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.450
So if we take one year of
data, Sentinel Satellite

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.760
covers the entire earth
approximately once every

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.110
five days.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.390
Let's now chop up the
entire earth into 512

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:44.100
by 512 tiles at
10-meter resolution

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.110
and then give a human
analyst 10 seconds

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.520
to look at each picture.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.410
That would take
them 130 years to go

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.860
through one year of satellite
data for one satellite.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.090
So obviously, that's silly.

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:55.844
We're not going to do that.

00:06:55.844 --> 00:06:57.510
But that kind of
illustrates the problem

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.480
of the old way of thinking
about satellite imagery analysis

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.100
and why we need new approaches.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.910
And then also for velocity,
thinking about the computation

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.170
that's required to handle
these types of data.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:13.730
So again, coming back to this
composite that we created,

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.890
this was made from
processing over

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:18.020
a petabyte of NASA imagery.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:20.210
And we did all this
processing within the Cloud

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.160
using preemptible VMs.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.070
And by spinning of
30,000 cores, we

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:31.220
were able to process and create
this image in under 16 hours.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:34.820
So this is actually even
larger than the throughput

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.530
that we need for the
current data sets,

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:38.180
but it demonstrates
the capability

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.400
that Google Cloud
will be able to scale

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:41.867
as the data that
I'm talking about

00:07:41.867 --> 00:07:43.700
continues to grow in
the way that we expect.

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.730
So to handle all of these
data, as I mentioned

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.790
at the beginning, we're building
a data refinery, or a platform,

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.370
to better understand our planet.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.100
And so what we want
to provide is ready

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:01.400
access to petabytes
of geospatial data

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:03.560
and then scalable
compute to back that up

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:06.375
and be able to create automated
and scalable analyses.

00:08:06.375 --> 00:08:09.740
And these are just a few
of the satellite sources

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:12.140
that we have on
platform right now.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:16.140
And this is going to
just continue to grow.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.320
So I've talked a lot about
all of this raw data.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.160
But what's really
important is that the value

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.240
lies in the information in
those pictures and the decisions

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.000
that you can make
from that information,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.370
not in the raw pixels.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.980
So it's important for
us to be able to sift

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:37.390
through all of that data and
arrive at decision points--

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:40.586
being able to monitor
something in supply chain

00:08:40.586 --> 00:08:42.169
or make a decision
about whether we're

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:44.061
going to go here or go there.

00:08:44.061 --> 00:08:46.560
So being able to pull together
all different types of data--

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.290
so here is [INAUDIBLE]
over Tokyo.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.840
You can see the ships
coming into port;

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.580
being able to understand
the changes over time--

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:57.740
this middle image are segmented
crop fields in Nebraska

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:01.630
that were created by looking
at detecting edges in images

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.950
and then looking at which
edges persist over time

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:06.209
to be able to tell
what fields are what;

00:09:06.209 --> 00:09:08.000
and then using machine
learning, which I'll

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.250
spend the second half of
my talk talking about,

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:15.290
to be able to analyze all
of these data at scale.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:19.580
And then what this gives you
is really a geospatial refinery

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:23.480
for business intelligence, so
again, taking all this data,

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:26.720
processing it, and then turning
into value and decisions

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:27.530
that you can use.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.770
So what the platform
actually looks

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:37.700
like, then, is effectively
a Python API for Earth,

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:38.850
is what we like to call it.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.580
So let's say, for
example, you want

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:45.050
to take-- we want to gather
Sentinel imagery over Taos

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.240
in northern New Mexico.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:50.090
If you were to start from
scratch trying to go directly

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.820
to the satellite source, this
would be quite challenging.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:54.630
But for us, it
only takes three--

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:56.960
four lines here--
importing the package,

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:59.630
specifying the
location, doing a search

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.660
over all of that giant
catalog of metadata

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:07.100
to find the Sentinel imagery
over this place in 2017

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:11.870
between March 12 and
March 20, and then

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.769
taking all those
numbers and burning them

00:10:13.769 --> 00:10:16.310
into an image or a picture that
you can actually see and work

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:17.730
with.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:21.290
So under the hood,
what we've provided

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:26.110
is fast access to over petabyte
of data on the platform.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:28.860
The search function for
searching through the metadata

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.560
is done with
Elasticsearch, and it

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.030
provides a millisecond response
times over all of those data.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:36.710
And then an automatic
tiling system,

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:39.470
so you don't have to worry
about putting together

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:42.060
different images and making sure
you register them correctly--

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:42.559
all this.

00:10:42.559 --> 00:10:46.340
And if you want more detail
about the underlying platform,

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.040
you can go back and watch
Tim's talk from yesterday,

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:52.946
[? IO263. ?]

00:10:52.946 --> 00:10:54.320
So the second half
of the talk, I

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:57.110
want to talk about the
machine learning approach

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:59.660
that we've taken to-- trying
to understand these data

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.920
and derive value from it.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.370
So I think another
important change

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.160
that at least the geospatial
data community is going through

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:10.490
right now is whereas
previously images were thought

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.830
of as something that a
human analyst could pull up

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:17.360
on their screen and then look
through and study and annotate,

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.120
with this massive amount
of data, as I mentioned,

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:21.480
that's no longer sufficient.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.540
And so we should start
thinking of these images

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.520
and all of these data
as input to computers,

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:28.310
input to algorithms, what
computers are seeing out

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:30.890
of this rather than what
humans are seeing out of this.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.390
And it really
changes how you think

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:35.090
about the type of analysis
and the type of questions

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:35.992
you can answer.

00:11:35.992 --> 00:11:37.700
And so that's what's
going to be required

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:42.410
to automate data analysis that
you can scale across the globe.

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.990
And then, this also
encourages us to simply think

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:48.800
differently about the types of
questions that one can answer.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:52.702
So hopefully, I can get you
all thinking through this talk.

00:11:52.702 --> 00:11:54.410
So a little bit about
what the input data

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:57.530
looks like from-- so now, me,
as a computer vision engineer,

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:59.570
I come in, and I
say, how am I going

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:02.940
to create a new useful tool?

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:05.540
So let's start with
the input data.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.300
As I mentioned previously, there
are several different types

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:09.260
of images.

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.900
The most common are
passive sensors, where

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:13.175
the sun is the light source.

00:12:13.175 --> 00:12:15.690
It shines, reflects off
the surface of the Earth,

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.900
and you take a picture
of that reflected light.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.900
So that's like
normal CCD cameras.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.660
Then, there are active sensors,
which are their own light

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:26.910
source, so like for example,
synthetic aperture radar,

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.600
which sends a radar
signal down, reflects it

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:31.760
off the surface of the
Earth, and then receives

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.880
that full wave
front, which it can

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:38.390
measure and interfere and
then use that to reconstruct

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:42.100
3D models of the ground.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:43.280
So we collect these data.

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.710
And many of the algorithms that
I'm going to be working with

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:50.700
are supervised algorithms.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.580
And so oftentimes, it'll
look like image target pairs.

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:55.680
So here, for
example, on the left,

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.920
I have a picture over a city
of high-resolution satellite

00:12:58.920 --> 00:12:59.760
imagery.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:02.310
And then on the right,
I have a labeled image

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:03.990
for where all the buildings are.

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:06.150
And so this is the
type of input data

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:10.290
that would go into
training an algorithm.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:14.670
Because the platform
is in Google Cloud,

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:18.480
it's trivial to be able to
process all of these data

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:21.270
and create these pairs
directly in the Cloud

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:22.950
and then save them
to Cloud Storage

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.350
and then be able to access
them seamlessly without having

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:30.140
to use any local resources.

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:32.720
For those of you who think
about analyzing imagery,

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.390
I just wanted to highlight
a few of the similarities

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:38.960
and differences between
processing normal imagery.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:40.730
So many things are
similar, but there

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:43.030
are some key
conceptual differences.

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:45.050
So in particular,
satellite imagery

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:48.110
usually has a known size
and scale with units.

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:52.340
Pixels usually correspond to
a specific size on the ground.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:55.370
In terms of data
augmentation, these images

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:57.414
are left, right, and
rotationally invariant.

00:13:57.414 --> 00:13:59.580
So you can flip them all
around to augment your data

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:02.540
sets easily.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:04.880
One of the key differences
is that often times,

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:06.597
we're interested
in finding things

00:14:06.597 --> 00:14:08.930
right at the limits of the
resolution of the imagery, so

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:11.240
small objects within images.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.420
Whereas in normal imagery,
whether it's the cheese example

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:17.510
that we saw from AutoML
that piece of cheese

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.379
takes up almost the entire
image, here we're looking for--

00:14:20.379 --> 00:14:21.920
you saw the buildings--
we're looking

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:23.210
for those little specks.

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:26.810
So in terms of thinking
about transfer learning,

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:28.770
the scales can be different.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:32.060
There are almost always
confusers in the data,

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:33.710
like clouds, haze, and shadows.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:36.050
So that requires a
lot of pre-processing.

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:39.980
And I'll show a little bit
later dealing with that.

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.310
And then in normal imagery,
there's usually three bands.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.140
Whereas in satellite imagery,
we can have an arbitrary number

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:46.640
of bands.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.407
For example, the
Sentinel Satellite

00:14:48.407 --> 00:14:50.240
from Europe that I've
referenced a few times

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:53.210
has 12 bands from the
visible all the way up

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:54.860
into thermal bands.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:59.570
And then a similarity that
works well with being--

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:02.600
working within the Cloud is that
these problems are generally

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.130
embarrassingly parallel.

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:06.650
You can take a full stack
of images in normal image

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.780
processing, or you
can tile up the earth

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:11.150
into a stack of images,
and then send them off

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:13.460
to an arbitrary
number of processors

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:17.890
to be able to run that--
run those jobs in parallel.

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:20.350
OK, so now we've
thought a little bit

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:21.430
about the input data.

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:24.590
How do we want to
analyze these data?

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:27.700
Well, there's three
sort of dimensions

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:30.880
that I like to think
about when thinking

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:33.100
about satellite imagery--
spatial, spectral,

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:34.360
and temporal.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:36.820
So spatial is just where
you are on the ground, so

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:40.240
the 2D context of where
you are on the earth

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:41.620
or within an image.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:44.140
Spectral are the different
wavelengths of light

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:47.320
that we record when we take
imagery in different bands.

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:50.380
And then temporal is for a
given location, obviously.

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:53.050
As you take pictures over
time, you can see what changes.

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:57.280
And so by combining
these different aspects

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.970
in different ways, we can access
different types of problems.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.470
So for example, computer
vision will usually

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:05.140
be combining spatial and
spectral information, where

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:08.320
you're trying to understand the
spatial context within an image

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:11.380
and then look for spectral
signatures of different types

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:14.800
of objects or land cover that
you want to be able to find

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.660
and map.

00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:18.940
And spectral and
temporal can often

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:21.050
be used to look for
and monitor change.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:24.770
So for example, looking at crop
fields over a growing season,

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:29.440
you can see how the plants are
growing and changing over time.

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:32.609
And then obviously, many
of the final solutions

00:16:32.609 --> 00:16:34.900
will be hybrid approaches
that will combine everything,

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:38.270
and I'll talk about one
of those at the end.

00:16:38.270 --> 00:16:42.490
So because we have chosen
to work within Google Cloud,

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:48.370
building these ML models is
a really efficient process.

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:50.980
So we work within
virtual machines

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:53.200
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:56.170
That allows us to easily
scale the resources

00:16:56.170 --> 00:17:01.550
that we need to process images,
train algorithms as needed.

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:04.750
And then, we're able to maintain
environmental consistency

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:06.369
from that development
and training

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:09.369
process all the way
through to deployment

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:11.740
by using Docker and Kubernetes.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:13.760
Because our data
live in Google Cloud,

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:18.800
it provides us super fast access
to that giant body of data.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:22.030
And then for training, we can
download smaller sets to VMs

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:24.640
or work with it
directly from the Cloud.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.660
And then we can deploy at scale.

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:28.810
So as I mentioned, most
of these are embarrass--

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:31.420
at the deployment stage,
most of these models

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:34.180
are an embarrassingly
parallel problem.

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:38.090
And so we can simply spawn up an
arbitrary number of processors.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:40.690
If I want to run a
model over the world

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:42.640
or over the United
States, I can just

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:44.800
spawn up that
number of processors

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:48.160
and get the results that
much faster, and then

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:51.280
results are saved
directly to the Cloud.

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:55.330
So from research
to building models

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.030
to deploying those models,
working within the Cloud

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:00.310
provides us an incredibly
efficient environment

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:02.870
for doing this work.

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:06.980
So now, I would like to tell
you about three different more

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:09.500
specific machine learning
approaches that we've taken

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.000
and the types of
problems that we've

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:12.884
been able to access with those.

00:18:12.884 --> 00:18:14.300
So I'll dig in a
little bit deeper

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.950
into the machine learning side
of the models we're building.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:19.610
So the first is
object detection.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:22.520
For those of you who are not
computer vision engineers,

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:24.080
the basic idea of
object detection

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:26.150
is exactly what those
words sound like.

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:28.310
You want to find objects.

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.350
And so for example,
here, we're looking

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:31.670
for electric substations.

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.900
So you want to find that object,
draw a box around it, and say,

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:36.740
there it is.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.810
The particular
implementation that we've

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:41.740
been working with so far is
called a Single Shot MultiBox

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:43.280
Detector, SSD.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:45.950
Single shot because it
takes a single forward pass

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:48.200
through the
architecture; MultiBox

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:52.110
because it uses a set
of predefined boxes

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:55.490
rather than dynamically
finding those boxes;

00:18:55.490 --> 00:18:59.790
and then detector because
it detects things.

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:02.160
How it works-- in one slide--

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:06.570
is this algorithm, as I said,
predefines a set of boxes.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:08.910
And then for each
of those boxes,

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:10.860
it uses a convolutional
neural net

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:12.940
to generate a set of features.

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:16.260
And then, it finally
predicts the competence of

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:19.360
whether or not that box
contains the object of interest,

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:22.950
so basically, it boils
this search problem down

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:24.550
into a classification problem.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:29.720
So here, this is from the paper
that defined this architecture.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:33.300
The blue box has the highest
probability of being a cat.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:36.780
And the red box has the highest
probability of being a dog.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:39.360
So we've taken that
and modified it

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:42.990
to work with satellite
imagery, and here's an example

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.120
of the results of that process.

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.540
So using OpenStreetMap
for the known locations

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:50.520
of electric
substations, we've been

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:53.250
able to train this
using NAIP imagery,

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:55.500
so that's the National
Agriculture Imagery Program

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:58.140
that covers the United States.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:01.200
This particular data set has
imagery at 60 centimeters

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:02.527
per pixel in four bands.

00:20:02.527 --> 00:20:04.110
And we've trained
this algorithm to be

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:07.270
able to find all the rest of
the substations in the United

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:09.990
States.

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.300
So that was one
example, and once we

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:15.420
have this setup with all the
imagery easily accessible

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:17.340
and the modeling
framework in place,

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:20.040
it's easy to train a
wide variety of models

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:21.725
across the industrial
supply chain

00:20:21.725 --> 00:20:23.100
to be able to find
these objects.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:25.110
So here are just two
examples of being

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:28.200
able to find storage tanks,
being able to find boats.

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:30.780
But you can just let
your imagination run wild

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:34.700
for what you would be
able to do with this.

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:37.670
The second class of models
that we worked with a lot

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:40.126
is called semantic segmentation.

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:46.309
So the basic idea with this--

00:20:46.309 --> 00:20:48.100
as opposed to object
detection where you're

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:50.050
trying to find
objects, here, you're

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:52.810
trying to say for every
pixel in an image,

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:56.500
is that pixel part
of a class or not?

00:20:56.500 --> 00:20:59.050
So the example on
the left hand side

00:20:59.050 --> 00:21:01.797
is that high-resolution
imagery, again, from NAIP.

00:21:01.797 --> 00:21:03.880
And then the right hand
side is the probability of

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:08.260
whether or not each pixel
is inside a building.

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:10.740
So similarly, how
it works-- here,

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:13.470
we define a deep
convolutional neural net.

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:17.310
And convolutional neural nets
are a particularly effective

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:19.230
architecture for these
types of analysis,

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.230
because they take into account
the two-dimensional context

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:24.540
of an image.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:26.550
So if you've ever
zoomed in on Google Maps

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:29.520
and then zoomed
way out, once you

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:30.979
zoom all the way
into pixel scales,

00:21:30.979 --> 00:21:33.186
it can be really hard to
tell what you're looking at.

00:21:33.186 --> 00:21:34.590
And you actually
need to zoom out

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:36.630
and be able to see
that spatial context.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:39.870
That's kind of conceptually
what convolutional neural nets

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:41.610
are able to capture.

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:44.400
So we define a deep
neural net architecture--

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:48.160
in this case, a U-Net, and then
create a bunch of these image

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:50.200
target pairs that I
mentioned at the beginning,

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:52.860
so in this case about
10,000 to 20,000 of these,

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:57.490
and then train the model
within the Cloud Platform.

00:21:57.490 --> 00:21:59.240
And this is what the
output looks like.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:03.290
So these are detections for
my model over LA County.

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:05.620
So you can see the
high-resolution imagery

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:08.180
on the far right hand side.

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:11.230
This is the raw
probability map output.

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:12.850
And then in the
middle, these have now

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:15.730
been segmented into
individual buildings.

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:18.010
And so what's
especially powerful here

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:20.980
is once we have this model,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:23.420
being able to run
this over large areas.

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:26.830
So I was able to map all
the buildings in California

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:30.210
by hitting Run,
going home to sleep.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:32.230
And then by the time I
got back in the morning,

00:22:32.230 --> 00:22:34.740
we had a map of all the
buildings in California.

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:38.910
And in principle, we can do
this over the entire world.

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:42.660
Another type of
segmentation that we've

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:45.870
been able to have success
with is using neural nets

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:47.310
to mask out clouds.

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:48.977
As you remember
at the beginning,

00:22:48.977 --> 00:22:51.060
I mentioned this is one
of the pernicious problems

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:55.720
with satellite imagery is
clouds exist, much to our bane--

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:58.470
the bane of our existence,
I would say rather.

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:02.430
And so by training a neural
net to find clouds and cloud

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:06.720
shadows, we can now
automatically mask out

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:10.620
these out of images and
then be able to remove

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:12.990
those parts from the analysis.

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:15.504
And this can always get better.

00:23:15.504 --> 00:23:16.920
This will be an
ongoing challenge.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:19.500
But this has been a
particularly successful approach

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:24.030
for being able to deal with
these types of non-idealities

00:23:24.030 --> 00:23:26.690
in the data.

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:29.910
Another approach is what I've
called here core segmentation.

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:34.580
So this is actually basically
a transfer learning approach,

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:37.900
where we start from a ResNet-50,
a pre-trained algorithm,

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:41.440
and then we tap into one of the
lower-resolution feature layers

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.540
and then simply add a
final classification

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:47.680
step to say whether or not
each of those coarser pixels

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:50.290
is the type of
thing-- in this case,

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:52.134
windmills-- that
we're looking for.

00:23:52.134 --> 00:23:53.800
And so we've taken
advantage of the fact

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:57.040
that ResNet's been able to
train on many, many, many, many

00:23:57.040 --> 00:23:58.840
images.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.180
And then with a relatively
small number of examples--

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:04.960
I think over 1,000-- a few
hundred to 1,000 examples

00:24:04.960 --> 00:24:05.590
here--

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:07.339
we've been able to
train an algorithm that

00:24:07.339 --> 00:24:08.820
can find and map windmills.

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:13.990
So this has been done across the
United States and across India.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:18.060
And then finally, it's
still semantic segmentation.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:21.580
But a bit of a simpler model
is simply pixel classification,

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:23.140
so taking that--
remember I mentioned

00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:26.380
the spectral information
within each pixel, what you see

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:28.600
in different bands,
and then feeding that

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:32.510
into a random forest classifier
and then just training it

00:24:32.510 --> 00:24:34.750
with a bunch of data
to say whether or not

00:24:34.750 --> 00:24:37.340
each pixel is land or water.

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:39.530
So this is in the Mekong Delta.

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:42.370
And you can see it clearly
gets the oceans and the rivers.

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:45.386
And then, there are a lot of
rice paddies in these areas,

00:24:45.386 --> 00:24:47.260
so that's kind of what
this speckling pattern

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:49.301
that you see throughout
the Mekong Delta is here.

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:54.150
A third type--
and this is fairly

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:57.060
different-- is what we
called visual search.

00:24:57.060 --> 00:24:59.830
So I'll demo this
in just a second.

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:04.530
But the idea is that this is
a website that you can go to.

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.260
You can click anywhere
either in the United States

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.040
or on the earth.

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:10.950
And it will simply
find other places

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:12.450
that are visually similar.

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:14.282
So let me go into a
little bit of detail

00:25:14.282 --> 00:25:16.740
on what's going on in the hood,
and then we can play around

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:19.470
with this a little bit.

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:25.740
So what's going on is that we've
taken the entire data set, so

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:26.460
the entire globe.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:28.860
We've chopped it up into
lots of little pieces.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.480
And then we feed
each of those pieces

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:31.950
through a convolutional
neural net

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:33.630
to produce a set of features.

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:36.210
And then we simply search for
nearest neighbors of features.

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:39.060
So in even more
detail than that,

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:41.490
we started from a
customized ResNet-50.

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:44.130
So started from ResNet-50
using all of that--

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:46.470
the training that had gone
into producing that model.

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:48.761
And then remember, I mentioned
that often times, you're

00:25:48.761 --> 00:25:50.250
looking for small
things in images

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:53.020
that are different from
normal image processing.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:55.980
So we fine-tuned trained
that with the OpenStreetMap

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:59.430
data set and then
also some unsupervised

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:01.380
autoencoding to
be able to produce

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:03.270
a useful set of features.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:06.300
We then encouraged those
features to either be 0 or 1,

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.640
so binarized that
set of features.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:12.360
And the result of that
process is that for each tile,

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:17.640
we get 512 binary bits, 0 or 1.

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:22.680
Then, we can simply search
for which sets of the features

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:27.414
are close to each other
using a Hamming distance.

00:26:27.414 --> 00:26:29.580
So we define that distance
using a Hamming distance,

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:32.280
and then we search for it
using an approximate hash-based

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:33.700
nearest neighbor search.

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:37.350
And so the result,
if I can switch over

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:41.060
to the other laptop--

00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:43.560
let's see if this-- oh yeah.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:48.020
OK, so this is now the
website running live.

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:52.141
And any of you can go to this
at search.descarteslabs.com.

00:26:52.141 --> 00:26:55.150
So first, let's play around
with the United States.

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:58.610
So this is this National
Agriculture Imagery Program

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:00.930
data set that I've
mentioned a few times.

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:04.370
And so I've highlighted
a few popular searches,

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:06.860
but let's click
on wind turbines.

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:10.130
So what this has done is
I've grabbed this tile

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:12.740
and then, like I said,
it is-- we have already

00:27:12.740 --> 00:27:17.960
indexed the entire United States
into these 512-bit long feature

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:18.679
vectors.

00:27:18.679 --> 00:27:20.970
And it simply searches for
other ones that are similar.

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:26.120
So it's pulled up a whole suite
of different wind turbines

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:29.960
all over the country, so some
here in the midwest, some

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:31.540
in the northeast.

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:34.940
And this is not a
specifically-trained model.

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:36.247
I didn't have to input--

00:27:36.247 --> 00:27:38.330
I didn't have to know
anything about wind turbines

00:27:38.330 --> 00:27:39.560
to be able to create this.

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:42.710
This is just simply searching
for visual similarity.

00:27:42.710 --> 00:27:46.730
Another one I thought was kind
of neat was the solar farms.

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:50.685
So it looks like this is a solar
farm maybe in Arizona or New

00:27:50.685 --> 00:27:51.185
Mexico.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:55.940
So click on that.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:58.140
And solar farms
look very similar,

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:02.390
so this does quite
well at picking up

00:28:02.390 --> 00:28:03.740
solar farm installations.

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:07.170
So let's then also look
at the entire Earth.

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:10.050
So we've done this with the
Landsat data set as well.

00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:15.080
And so same idea, being able
to index the entire globe.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:18.812
So pivot irrigation are
very unique signatures.

00:28:18.812 --> 00:28:20.520
I can click on that,
and all of a sudden,

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:23.120
I can see pivot
irrigation in Australia;

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:26.930
over Africa, the sub-Saharan;
in the United States;

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:30.630
down in South America.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:36.540
So it's a very powerful tool to
be able to effectively search

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:37.890
the entire globe for things.

00:28:37.890 --> 00:28:41.260
So you can go to this
today and say, huh,

00:28:41.260 --> 00:28:44.910
I want to look for
railway stations that

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:46.680
look similar to the
one near my house.

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.720
Let's click on
that and see where

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:51.010
else they are around the world.

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:54.200
And so this is this
incredibly fast search.

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:57.540
And so you can see how quickly
we're able to zoom around.

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:59.610
This is all enabled
by having the platform

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:01.470
built within the Cloud.

00:29:01.470 --> 00:29:07.396
So if I can go back
to my talk slides.

00:29:07.396 --> 00:29:07.896
Perfect.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:16.270
So with those two
approaches, there--

00:29:16.270 --> 00:29:19.480
we basically have a two-stage
approach to performing searches

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:24.310
when we say we want to try
to find some type of thing.

00:29:24.310 --> 00:29:27.610
Because of the capabilities
of the geovisual search,

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:30.370
we can do a low-capacity
quick search

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:33.070
for things like
the wind turbines.

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:34.330
Just click on that.

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:36.619
Or build out a
small model and say,

00:29:36.619 --> 00:29:39.160
let's just try to find, roughly,
where some wind turbines are

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:42.610
and differentiate them from corn
fields or forests or whatever.

00:29:42.610 --> 00:29:45.700
And then if that's enough
for your particular solution,

00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:47.710
super cheap, super
fast and easy.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:50.170
You're done, no work.

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:51.940
Then, obviously,
many solutions will

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:53.380
require much higher precisions.

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:55.070
Like mapping out
buildings, for example,

00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:56.270
wouldn't work with that.

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:59.800
And so then we can develop
a higher capacity model that

00:29:59.800 --> 00:30:02.900
takes more time and effort.

00:30:02.900 --> 00:30:06.460
And so that's the examples I
showed with buildings or wind

00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:11.270
turbines or power substations.

00:30:11.270 --> 00:30:13.499
And then, of course,
the last type

00:30:13.499 --> 00:30:15.290
of models I want to
talk a little bit about

00:30:15.290 --> 00:30:17.360
are hybrid models and
an example of something

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:20.430
we've done that sort of pulls
all these pieces together.

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:24.170
And so what this is, is
one of the original models

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:25.790
that Descartes Labs
produced, which

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:29.180
is the Corn Forecasting Model.

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:32.270
So the goal is to be able
to forecast corn production

00:30:32.270 --> 00:30:35.030
for the United States.

00:30:35.030 --> 00:30:37.724
So first, as I
mentioned earlier,

00:30:37.724 --> 00:30:39.140
using the temporal
information, we

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:41.300
can segment out which--
where different fields are

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:42.591
and what those field types are.

00:30:42.591 --> 00:30:44.660
So how this actually
works underneath

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:49.430
is because we have time series
imagery over the United States,

00:30:49.430 --> 00:30:52.190
we can just use normal image
processing to find the edges.

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:54.800
It's just an edge detection
within those images.

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:57.800
And then, we can look at
which edges persist over time

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:04.340
and use that as a way to
segment out field boundaries.

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:06.110
Then once we have
those field boundaries,

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:07.860
it becomes a
classification problem.

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:10.370
So taking into
account the spectral

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:12.320
and the spatial
context information,

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:13.910
we can classify those fields.

00:31:13.910 --> 00:31:16.880
Here, these are
either corn or soy--

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:18.590
corn in yellow and soy in green.

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:20.660
And what you're
actually seeing here

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:24.440
is the field
classification released

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:26.370
by the US Department
of Agriculture

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:29.760
with a lot of measurements
on the ground.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:33.020
And what you see here is
what Descartes released

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:34.590
six months ahead of time.

00:31:34.590 --> 00:31:37.610
So what you should
say is, hey, look.

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:40.130
Those look pretty much the same.

00:31:40.130 --> 00:31:43.040
And they should, because the
US Department of Agriculture

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.690
accuracy is quite good.

00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:46.940
The key is that we were able
to do this in effectively

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:49.640
real time rather than
having to wait six months.

00:31:49.640 --> 00:31:51.260
And then the final
step of that model

00:31:51.260 --> 00:31:55.800
is a regression problem, so
saying we have historical data.

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:58.760
We know how much corn
production-- corn

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:00.630
was produced in previous years.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:03.350
So let's run this model
and fit to a model

00:32:03.350 --> 00:32:05.930
to predict how much
corn can be produced.

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:08.240
And so you put all of
those pieces together,

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:12.350
and what the graph on
the right is showing

00:32:12.350 --> 00:32:15.920
is the y-axis is the
uncertainty on how well you

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:17.930
have predicted corn.

00:32:17.930 --> 00:32:20.960
So small uncertainty is
good, so down is good.

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:22.520
And then as a function of time--

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:23.894
so you can see on
the-- as you go

00:32:23.894 --> 00:32:26.900
to the far edge of the graph,
so you extend the time,

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:29.700
they approached
the same accuracy.

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:34.200
But the blue Descartes Labs's
points got there way earlier.

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:38.090
And so this gives
businesses and customers

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:40.940
real actionable
insight that they can

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:45.030
turn into real value in money.

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:50.190
So wrapping that
up, if any of you

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:52.470
are interested in
building models

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:56.720
or if this brings up ideas, feel
free to come up and talk to me.

00:32:56.720 --> 00:32:58.680
There's information
on our website.

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:02.980
And then, you can email either
of these email addresses.

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:06.540
And I'd love to talk to you more
about what types of problems

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:09.420
you might be able to approach
with these types of data

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.850
or more questions about how
we've been able to build out

00:33:11.850 --> 00:33:16.350
all this infrastructure and our
platform using Google Cloud.

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:18.120
So with that, thank
you all for coming.

00:33:18.120 --> 00:33:21.470
